              "content": "<table><tr><td>Process</td><td>SHG</td><td>THG</td><td>SD</td></tr><tr><td><eq>\\mathcal{N}[E]</eq></td><td><eq>E^{2}</eq></td><td><eq>E^{3}</eq></td><td><eq>|E|^{2}E</eq></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.355,
            0.483,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Expressions for the processes commonly used in pulse measurement can be found in Table 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.385,
            0.486,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "PNPS traces do not uniquely define a pulse. For example, they are all ambiguous to the constant and linear phase of  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{E}(\\omega)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Some methods (e.g., SHG-FROG and SHG-iFROG) leave the direction of time undetermined. Additionally, the relative phase of pulse components well-separated in frequency can be shown to be ambiguous, similar to how it was done for FROG [42]. Answering the underlying question if a PNPS trace is essentially unique, i.e., if it defines pulse amplitude and phase up to a set of known, so-called trivial ambiguities, is out of scope for this work. Even for the well-studied FROG method, it is still a topic of ongoing research [7,8,43]. We will take a pragmatic approach and test for nontrivial ambiguities by numerically retrieving pulses from a large number of synthetic measurements."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.585,
            0.288,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "B. Discrete PNPS Formalism"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.604,
            0.484,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To perform pulse retrieval, we have to introduce a discrete version of the PNPS formalism. We define equidistant simulation grids with N points in time and frequency,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.66,
            0.481,
            0.676
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "t _ {n} \\equiv t _ {0} + n \\Delta t, \\quad n = 0, \\dots , N - 1,\\tag{5}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.218,
            0.695,
            0.481,
            0.71
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "\\omega_ {n} \\equiv \\omega_ {0} + n \\Delta \\omega .\\tag{6}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.721,
            0.483,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We set  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "E_{n} \\equiv E(t_{n})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{E}_{n} \\equiv \\tilde{E}(\\omega_{n})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We use  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf{E} \\equiv (E_{0}, \\ldots, E_{N-1})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{\\mathbf{E}} \\equiv (\\tilde{E}_{0}, \\ldots, \\tilde{E}_{N-1})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  to denote the whole pulse. The Fourier transform is approximated by discrete evaluation of the integral and is denoted by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.79,
            0.481,
            0.809
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\tilde {E} _ {n} = \\mathrm{FT} _ {k \\to n} (E _ {k}) \\quad \\text {and} \\quad E _ {k} = \\mathrm{FT} _ {n \\to k} ^ {- 1} (\\tilde {E} _ {n}).\\tag{7}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.817,
            0.484,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have M spectra for the parameters  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\delta_{0},\\ldots,\\delta_{M-1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . There is no restriction on the number, spacing, or position of the tuning parameters  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\delta_{m}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , e.g., as it is required by PCGPA for FROG. The discrete PNPS signal  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "S_{mk}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  is defined by a discrete evaluation of the signal operator at  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\delta_{m}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{k}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.905,
            0.481,
            0.933
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "S _ {m k} \\equiv S _ {m k} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) \\approx \\mathcal {S} _ {\\delta_ {m}} [ \\tilde {E} ] (t _ {k}), \\qquad \\begin{array}{l} m = 0, \\dots , M - 1 \\\\ k = 0, \\dots , N - 1 \\end{array} .\\tag{8}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.514,
            0.058,
            0.874,
            0.074
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Its counterpart in the frequency domain is denoted by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.535,
            0.083,
            0.926,
            0.113
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{r l r}\\tilde {S} _ {m n} \\equiv \\tilde {S} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) = \\mathrm{FT} _ {k \\rightarrow n} (S _ {m k}),&m = 0, \\dots , M - 1\\\\&n = 0, \\dots , N - 1.\\end{array}\\tag{9}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.514,
            0.12,
            0.886,
            0.138
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finally, we can calculate the discrete PNPS trace "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.579,
            0.14,
            0.927,
            0.16
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_23.jpg",
          "content": "\\tilde {T} _ {m n} \\equiv \\tilde {T} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) = | \\tilde {S} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) | ^ {2} \\approx T (\\delta_ {m}, \\omega_ {n}; \\tilde {E}).\\tag{10}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.163,
            0.931,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The measurement from which the pulse is reconstructed, the measured PNPS trace, is denoted by  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . More details on how the calculations are performed can be found in the Supplement 1 (Section S2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.514,
            0.247,
            0.789,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. PULSE RETRIEVAL PROBLEM"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.512,
            0.268,
            0.931,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The discrete pulse retrieval problem is to find the pulse  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{E}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  that gives rise to a PNPS trace  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  that matches the measurement  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . As  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  is subject to measurement errors; the retrieval will never be exact, and we need to choose a metric to select the best solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.345,
            0.931,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this work, we view pulse retrieval as a nonlinear least squares problem. It is a nonlinear inverse problem, and solving such problems in the least squares sense is very well-established and understood [44]. Under the assumption of Gaussian measurement errors, the least squares solution represents an optimal choice, namely the maximum-likelihood estimate [45], and a Gaussian distribution is usually a good model for noise in spectrometric measurements [46]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 28,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.462,
            0.931,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To explain why we put so much emphasis on finding a least squares solution, we have to anticipate a key result from Section 6.B. We found that retrieval algorithms based on generalized projections or ptychography do not converge well onto a solution in the least squares sense. In our numerical tests, we found that they stagnate before reaching the close proximity of the least squares solution. In consequence, they underperform in the presence of additive Gaussian noise. This fact has been noticed several times in the literature  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "[11,24,31,47]"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , but the relation to the missing least squares property was not reported so far."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 29,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.609,
            0.931,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "With this in mind, we state the pulse retrieval problem as fitting the 2N independent variables in  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{E}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  (real and imaginary parts) to MN dependent variables in  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  by minimizing the sum of squared residuals r,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 30,
          "bbox": [
            0.606,
            0.675,
            0.927,
            0.705
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_30.jpg",
          "content": "r \\equiv r (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) = \\sum_ {m, n} [ \\tilde {T} _ {m n} ^ {\\text {meas}} - \\mu \\tilde {T} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) ] ^ {2}.\\tag{11}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 31,
          "bbox": [
            0.515,
            0.711,
            0.9,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Throughout the paper, we use the trace error "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "R"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " defined by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 32,
          "bbox": [
            0.59,
            0.735,
            0.927,
            0.759
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_32.jpg",
          "content": "R \\equiv R (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) = r ^ {1 / 2} / [ M N (\\max _ {m, n} \\tilde {T} _ {m n} ^ {\\mathrm{meas}}) ^ {2} ] ^ {1 / 2}\\tag{12}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 33,
          "bbox": [
            0.514,
            0.765,
            0.931,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "to assess the convergence. R is the normalized root mean square error (NRMSE) between  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mu\\tilde{T}_{mn}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . The normalization facilitates the comparison of R between different measurements and PNPS schemes. In the FROG literature, the maximum of  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{T}_{mn}^{meas}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  is usually normalized to unity and, thus, R is then equivalent to the FROG error G."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 34,
          "bbox": [
            0.513,
            0.855,
            0.931,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The scaling factor  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mu"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  in Eq. (11) accounts for different scales of the measured and computed traces. Its value can be obtained for every  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\tilde{E}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  from an analytical solution by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 35,
          "bbox": [
            0.599,
            0.903,
            0.927,
            0.934
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_35.jpg",
          "content": "\\mu = \\sum_ {m, n} [ \\tilde {T} _ {m n} ^ {\\text {meas}} \\tilde {T} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) ] / \\sum_ {m, n} \\tilde {T} _ {m n} (\\tilde {\\mathbf {E}}) ^ {2}.\\tag{13}"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "is_full_document": false,
  "schema": "docvortex.middle",
  "schema_version": "2.0"
}